  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nH5 ridging generally remains intact through the beginning of this\nweekend, which will keep a surface high pressure system in control of\nour weather. Given the anomalous heights in the ridge, temperatures\nwill continue to trend well above normal values today through Sunday\nunder a southerly flow regime. Given that the air mass still remains\npretty dry, overnight lows will be able to drop into the middle 30s \ninto the middle 40s through Saturday night, resulting in a 25-30+ \ndegree swing in diurnal temperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nThe H5 ridge will finally begin to propagate eastward during the\nlatter half of the weekend, eventually replaced by an amplified\ntrough. A cold front will swing through the Ohio Valley Sunday \nnight, which will usher in some rain late Sunday into Sunday night. \nQPF values generally remain pretty low, with most of the area likely \nobserving 0.25\" or less based on latest guidance. \n\nBehind the front, a cooler air mass looks to remain in place at least\ninto the Thanksgiving holiday. High temperatures will generally be\nback in the 40s next week. It should also be mentioned that renewed\nchances for precipitation are possible Wednesday into Thursday. With\nthe cooler thermal profiles, cannot rule out the possibility for some\nsnowflakes to mix in with the rain.",
